---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Mission" description="amber mission: build an agent runtime that stays understandable as agents become more capable.">
  <NavBar />
  <main>
    <h1>Mission</h1>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>The North Star</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Build an agent runtime that stays understandable as agents become more
        capable.
      </p>
      <p>
        As models get stronger, the harness around them matters more, not less.
        A capable model in a tangled, hardcoded runtime is harder to trust
        than a weaker model in an explicit one. Amber's mission is to keep the
        runtime legible, so you can understand what the agent is doing, why,
        and where its boundaries are.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider-thick" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>How</h2>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li><strong>Native runtime</strong>: compiled C++17 from the agent loop up, not a collection of scripts around an API. One process, no interpreter dependency, the core usable as a library.</li>
        <li><strong>Explicit capabilities</strong>: providers, tools and UI extensions arrive through the same plugin architecture. The core does not need to know every integration that exists; new ones do not require special cases throughout the engine.</li>
        <li><strong>Model / provider separation</strong>: the agent runtime is independent of the model provider. Provider integrations are plugins, so the harness works with different vendors and OpenAI-compatible endpoints without changing the agent loop.</li>
        <li><strong>Extensibility</strong>: plugins can be enabled, disabled and unwound cleanly, with contributions tracked by owner. The architecture is shaped for runtime loading, even if the current set is compiled in.</li>
        <li><strong>Measurable behavior</strong>: amber-bench runs repeatable scenarios against the same engine and produces KPI scores. Harness changes are tested against the corpus, not judged from demos.</li>
        <li><strong>Explicit trust boundaries</strong>: model output is treated as untrusted input. Filesystem operations are confined, side-effecting operations can require approval, shell execution is gated. The goal is not to pretend an agent is safe by default, but to make its trust boundaries explicit.</li>
        <li><strong>Open source</strong>: Apache 2.0, for everyone, without discrimination. Ready for people to try, break, inspect and contribute to.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>The Spirit</h2>
      <p>
        An interface inspired by BitchX and the golden age of IRC. Fast,
        keyboard-driven, dense, beautiful. The drawer pops on <code>/</code>,
        filters as you type, and <code>/q</code>, <code>/sl</code> become
        muscle memory again. The interface anticipates. It helps. It gets out
        of your way when you know what you are doing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Who It Is For</h2>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li><strong>Developers</strong>: from a single file to a monorepo</li>
        <li><strong>System administrators</strong>: server management, automation, deployment</li>
        <li><strong>Security professionals</strong>: auditing, tooling, long-running investigations</li>
        <li><strong>Researchers</strong>: data analysis, experiments that run for days</li>
        <li><strong>Anyone</strong> who lives in a terminal and wants an AI partner</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Amber runs on Linux and macOS. It is free and open source (Apache
        2.0), for everyone, without discrimination. If you live in a terminal,
        it is yours.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .section p.lead {
    color: #00ff41;
    font-size: 1.4em;
    font-weight: bold;
  }
</style>
